`cast` performs an explicit conversion to a compiler-supported builtin target.

The target comes from the immediate typed receiving boundary. Every numeric type
spelling is a valid target where its source-target policy allows the pair.

Moth adds no numeric conversion syntax. There is no parenthesised target, no
target after the expression and no constructor-like conversion form.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    octet U8 = 42
    count U64 = cast octet
    half F16 = cast octet
    raw Byte = cast octet
    money Dec2 = cast count
]

Each conversion above is infallible: `U8` fits `U64`, `Byte` and the `F16`
finite range, and a whole number scales exactly into `Dec2`. Fixed-width and
`Byte` cast policies and evidence are implemented in the frontend; the
frontend also implements compiler-owned `Dec` cast policies. HTML-JS
executes fixed-width, `Byte` and `Dec` runtime casts. Its exercised
`Dec` casts include infallible integer-to-`Dec` and scale widening; scale
narrowing and `Dec`-to-integer conversions are exact and fallible.
`String`-to-`Dec` parsing is fallible and consumes the whole existing
numeric grammar exactly; `Dec`-to-`String` uses canonical formatting.

HTML-Wasm executes infallible numeric expression casts that do not involve
`F16`, as well as integer-to-`F16` and widening `F16`-to-`F32`/`F64`, plus
`Byte -> U8` and `U8 -> Byte`. F32/F64-to-F16 narrowing and other
numeric-to-numeric casts from F16 remain target-gated. It supports infallible
integer-to-`String` expression casts from profile `Int` and fixed
signed/unsigned integers. `Float`/`F16`/`F32`/`F64`-to-`String` formatting is
a separate integrity guard, not implicit numeric failure: it halts on a
non-finite input unless a declared builtin `Error!` contract returns it.

HTML-Wasm rejects reachable `Dec` values and signatures, including nested
types and generated-function uses; unreachable private helpers do not gate
the target. On HTML-Wasm, remaining fallible casts, `String` parsing,
conversions involving `Bool`, `Char` or `Error` (including `Char -> Int`),
statement-form casts, source-level external `Float` imports and their
finite-boundary validation, and reachable `ReturnError` `FormatFloat`/`ValidateFloat` remain target-rejected.
See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    parse_count |text String| -> Int, Error!:
        return cast! text
    ;

    ratio Float = cast 3
    label String = cast ratio
]

### Forms

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value Target = cast expression
    value Target = cast! expression

    value Target = cast expression catch:
        then fallback
    ;

    value Target = cast expression catch |err|:
        then fallback
    ;
]

- Plain `cast` without an enclosing handler requires infallible evidence.
- `cast!` requires fallible evidence and explicitly propagates through a compatible builtin `Error!` slot, including `start` at the top level.
- `cast ... catch:` recovers the complete selected expression, including operand evaluation and the conversion. A fallible conversion needs fallible evidence; an infallible conversion can still have a failing operand.
- Cast syntax does not contain the target in parentheses or after the expression.

Implicit failure and builtin `Error!` can share the cast expression's handler.
`cast!` and `catch` on the same cast remain invalid. Catch does not legalise
invalid cast evidence. See @./#fallible-casts (Fallible casts).

### Removed conversion constructors

These scalar conversion forms are invalid:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Int(value)
    Float(value)
    Bool(value)
    String(value)
    Char(value)
]

Every numeric type name follows the same rule. `U8(value)`, `U64(value)`,
`F16(value)`, `Byte(value)` and `Dec2(value)` are a type name applied to an
argument, never a conversion.

`Error(...)` remains a real builtin constructor:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    err = Error("Missing number", 200)
]

Conversion to `Error` still uses `cast` when conversion rather than construction
is intended:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    err Error = cast "Missing number"
]
